use ../../../lib/UiComponent

export ui-component Button:
    description:
        A button does something when the user presses it. It shows a label, an
        icon, or both. It can be disabled so it can't be pressed, or show that
        it's busy while its action is running.

    content:
        A label, an icon, or both. The icon can go before or after the label.
        A button always has a name that assistive tools can read out, even if
        it shows no text.

    activation:
        The user presses a button the usual way for the platform: clicking,
        tapping, or using the keyboard. One full press is one activation. The
        user can back out partway, such as by dragging off before letting go,
        and nothing happens. Which keys press it, and whether it fires on the
        way down or on release, is up to the chosen library.

    repeatedActivation:
        Every full press counts, however quickly they come. None are merged or
        ignored. If the component using the button must not act twice, it puts
        the button into its busy state.

    disabled:
        A disabled button can't be pressed, and it's clear to the user that
        it's unavailable. Whether it can still receive focus is up to the
        chosen library.

    pending:
        The busy state is turned on and off by the component using the button.
        Pressing the button never turns it on by itself. A busy button can't be
        pressed, and it's clear to the user that it's working. Its label and
        name stay the same, and focus doesn't move when it starts or stops
        being busy. How "busy" is shown is up to the chosen library.

    nonGoals:
        - Buttons don't go anywhere. Something that takes the user to another
            place is a Link, even if it looks like a button
        - Double-clicking or long-pressing doesn't mean anything special
